1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,207
A loop that works on a
good day is the easy part.

2
00:00:04,557 --> 00:00:10,312
A good agent also behaves well on a
bad day: when it can't finish, when a tool

3
00:00:10,312 --> 00:00:13,115
fails, or when the input is dangerous.

4
00:00:13,465 --> 00:00:15,967
This lesson is about stopping safely.

5
00:00:16,317 --> 00:00:20,173
These habits will protect
every agent you build from now on.

6
00:00:21,973 --> 00:00:24,701
You'll list every way
an agent loop can end.

7
00:00:25,301 --> 00:00:29,082
You'll return tool errors to
the model, instead of crashing.

8
00:00:29,682 --> 00:00:32,849
And you'll build tools
that refuse dangerous input.

9
00:00:34,649 --> 00:00:37,957
A well-built agent
knows exactly why it stopped.

10
00:00:38,307 --> 00:00:41,975
Here are the six ways
our calculator agent can end.

11
00:00:42,575 --> 00:00:45,399
First, the good
ending: the model answered.

12
00:00:45,999 --> 00:00:47,357
Second, the step limit.

13
00:00:47,707 --> 00:00:52,853
After a fixed number of turns, we
stop, even if the model wants to continue.

14
00:00:53,453 --> 00:00:55,815
Third, the budget, from Module 2.

15
00:00:56,165 --> 00:00:58,717
When spending reaches the limit, we stop.

16
00:00:59,317 --> 00:01:02,995
Fourth, a refusal: the
model declined the request.

17
00:01:03,595 --> 00:01:06,462
Fifth, the reply was
cut off at max tokens.

18
00:01:07,062 --> 00:01:12,338
And sixth, an API error that
retries couldn't fix, like a rejected key.

19
00:01:12,938 --> 00:01:16,185
For each ending, the
user gets one clear message.

20
00:01:16,535 --> 00:01:19,528
Never a traceback,
and never a silent stop.

21
00:01:21,328 --> 00:01:23,645
Why does the step limit matter so much?

22
00:01:23,995 --> 00:01:27,867
A model can get stuck,
asking for tools again and again.

23
00:01:28,217 --> 00:01:33,750
Our fake API has a mode that does
exactly that, so we can watch the limit work.

24
00:01:34,350 --> 00:01:36,822
The fake keeps asking for one plus one.

25
00:01:37,172 --> 00:01:42,900
After three turns, the agent stops and
explains why, instead of running, and costing

26
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:43,923
money, forever.

27
00:01:45,723 --> 00:01:47,150
Next, tool errors.

28
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:53,874
Tools will fail sometimes: the input is
invalid, there's a division by zero, or a

29
00:01:53,874 --> 00:01:55,369
unit doesn't exist.

30
00:01:55,969 --> 00:01:57,922
Don't let that crash your program.

31
00:01:58,272 --> 00:02:02,781
Instead, send the error message
back to the model, as the tool's result.

32
00:02:03,381 --> 00:02:07,478
Mark it with is error, true, so
the model knows the call failed.

33
00:02:08,078 --> 00:02:12,516
Now the model can read the
error, fix its input, and try again.

34
00:02:12,866 --> 00:02:16,245
And your step limit still
caps how many times it can try.

35
00:02:18,045 --> 00:02:19,869
Here's how the project does it.

36
00:02:20,219 --> 00:02:23,577
Act runs one tool call
and records it as a step.

37
00:02:24,177 --> 00:02:27,392
If the tool raises a
tool error, we don't crash.

38
00:02:27,742 --> 00:02:33,262
The error message becomes the
observation, marked as an error, and the loop carries

39
00:02:33,262 --> 00:02:33,461
on.

40
00:02:35,261 --> 00:02:41,298
Here the fake is scripted to send a
broken expression first: eighteen x one point

41
00:02:41,298 --> 00:02:41,969
one five.

42
00:02:42,319 --> 00:02:46,659
The calculator returns an
error, shown with an exclamation mark.

43
00:02:47,009 --> 00:02:51,526
On the next turn the expression is
fixed, and the agent finishes normally.

44
00:02:51,876 --> 00:02:55,128
With a real model, this
recovery isn't scripted.

45
00:02:55,478 --> 00:03:00,810
The model reads the error message and
corrects itself, which is why clear error

46
00:03:00,810 --> 00:03:01,890
messages matter.

47
00:03:03,690 --> 00:03:06,795
Now the most important
safety rule in this lesson.

48
00:03:07,145 --> 00:03:12,646
Tool input comes from the model, and
the model's input comes, partly, from users.

49
00:03:12,996 --> 00:03:16,218
So treat tool input
like any untrusted input.

50
00:03:16,568 --> 00:03:22,540
A calculator built with Python's eval
function looks clever, but eval runs any

51
00:03:22,540 --> 00:03:25,603
Python code at all, not just arithmetic.

52
00:03:26,203 --> 00:03:31,801
Instead, our calculator parses the
expression into a tree, and walks through it,

53
00:03:31,801 --> 00:03:36,000
allowing only numbers, a few
operators, and three functions.

54
00:03:36,350 --> 00:03:38,105
Anything else is refused.

55
00:03:39,905 --> 00:03:43,558
This script shows the
difference, with a harmless example.

56
00:03:43,908 --> 00:03:49,854
Given this text, eval happily imports
the os module and runs a function: here it

57
00:03:49,854 --> 00:03:51,787
prints the current folder.

58
00:03:52,137 --> 00:03:54,778
It could just as easily delete files.

59
00:03:55,128 --> 00:04:00,133
Our calculate function refuses it,
and still does real maths perfectly.

60
00:04:01,933 --> 00:04:03,150
Here's the idea.

61
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:07,268
Each kind of node in the
tree must be explicitly allowed.

62
00:04:07,618 --> 00:04:11,187
Numbers are allowed, and
so are plus and minus signs.

63
00:04:11,787 --> 00:04:17,613
Arithmetic operators are allowed, but
only the ones in our list, and very large

64
00:04:17,613 --> 00:04:23,439
powers are refused, so nobody can
freeze your program with a giant calculation.

65
00:04:24,039 --> 00:04:29,025
Three named functions are allowed:
square root, absolute value, and round.

66
00:04:29,625 --> 00:04:31,475
Everything else is refused.

67
00:04:31,825 --> 00:04:36,024
That's an allow list: you list
what's permitted, and refuse the rest.

68
00:04:36,374 --> 00:04:39,983
It's much safer than trying to
list everything that's dangerous.

69
00:04:41,783 --> 00:04:42,846
One more thing.

70
00:04:43,196 --> 00:04:49,048
In real projects, the Anthropic SDK has
a tool runner that can run this loop for

71
00:04:49,048 --> 00:04:49,340
you.

72
00:04:49,940 --> 00:04:55,124
We built the loop by hand, so you
understand exactly what any tool runner does

73
00:04:55,124 --> 00:04:55,855
underneath.

74
00:04:56,455 --> 00:05:02,505
In Module 5, you'll add more tools,
validate their inputs, and ask a human for

75
00:05:02,505 --> 00:05:05,142
confirmation before risky actions.

76
00:05:05,742 --> 00:05:11,447
And in Module 7, you'll make agents
reliable, with timeouts, token budgets, logging,

77
00:05:11,447 --> 00:05:12,261
and tracing.

78
00:05:14,061 --> 00:05:18,001
Pause and match each problem
to the protection that handles it.

79
00:05:18,601 --> 00:05:19,240
One.

80
00:05:19,590 --> 00:05:23,197
The model keeps asking
for tools, and never answers.

81
00:05:23,797 --> 00:05:24,419
Two.

82
00:05:24,769 --> 00:05:28,421
A user tries to sneak
Python code into the calculator.

83
00:05:29,021 --> 00:05:29,718
Three.

84
00:05:30,068 --> 00:05:34,215
The model sends a letter x
instead of a multiplication sign.

85
00:05:39,815 --> 00:05:41,155
One: the step limit.

86
00:05:41,505 --> 00:05:46,840
Two: the allow-list calculator, which
refuses anything that isn't arithmetic.

87
00:05:47,190 --> 00:05:51,474
Three: the tool error goes back
to the model, which fixes its input.

88
00:05:53,274 --> 00:05:54,133
Quick check.

89
00:05:54,483 --> 00:05:59,189
Why send a tool's error back to the
model, instead of raising an exception?

90
00:06:05,389 --> 00:06:09,089
So the model can read it,
fix its input, and continue.

91
00:06:09,439 --> 00:06:12,462
And the step limit still
caps how often it can try.

92
00:06:14,262 --> 00:06:18,424
A loop can end six ways,
and each gets one clear message.

93
00:06:19,024 --> 00:06:21,177
A step limit stops endless loops.

94
00:06:21,777 --> 00:06:25,447
Tool errors are
observations, marked with is error.

95
00:06:26,047 --> 00:06:28,014
And never eval tool input.

96
00:06:28,364 --> 00:06:29,762
Use an allow list.

97
00:06:30,362 --> 00:06:33,286
You now know every idea the project needs.

98
00:06:33,636 --> 00:06:37,265
Next lesson, we build
the smart calculator agent.
